  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14155061/assets/24e777c7b9029007debe066de66ea7041b96d02702788bfea4d2da4914a47da6.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{4}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
